    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so just to make sure i get it right... in the AC version, is the capacitor actually letting charge flow through it, or is it just the electric field changing that makes it look like current is moving? and does the frequency affect how much resistance (reactance) it gives? like if the frequency gets really high, what happens to the reactance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's more accurate to say that in an AC circuit, the capacitor is constantly charging and discharging as the voltage alternates, which is what we interpret as current flow. It's not that the charge is permanently flowing through the dielectric material, which is a very good insulator. The frequency *absolutely* affects the reactance; as the frequency gets higher, the capacitive reactance ($X_C$) gets smaller, meaning the capacitor lets more current through. This is because the capacitor has less time to build up a large opposing charge when the voltage is rapidly switching.",
      "source": "target",
